1
00:00:00,540 --> 00:00:05,776
In February 2026, the average 30-year
fixed mortgage rate in

2
00:00:05,776 --> 00:00:11,100
the US fell to 5.98 percent, the lowest
since September 2022.

3
00:00:12,015 --> 00:00:16,345
Nora had borrowed in 2023, at 7.62
percent.

4
00:00:17,165 --> 00:00:22,725
At the February rate, a new loan would
have cut her payment by $459 a month.

5
00:00:23,645 --> 00:00:24,645
She didn't take it.

6
00:00:25,425 --> 00:00:29,211
By late September, the rate was back above
7 percent,

7
00:00:29,211 --> 00:00:31,925
for the first time since January 2025.

8
00:00:33,430 --> 00:00:38,219
Now a letter comes from her lender: an
offer to refinance,

9
00:00:38,219 --> 00:00:44,990
a new loan at a slightly lower rate that
pays off the old one, for $5,124 in fees.

10
00:00:46,495 --> 00:00:51,115
How big a rate cut makes that worth it for
Nora — and where would your own loan fall?

11
00:00:51,895 --> 00:00:54,465
And why would a smaller mortgage need a
bigger cut?

12
00:00:56,020 --> 00:01:01,042
Nora borrowed in October 2023, the month
the average

13
00:01:01,042 --> 00:01:05,870
rate reached 7.79 percent, its highest
since 2000.

14
00:01:06,775 --> 00:01:11,130
She wasn't the only one: three in ten of
that year's 30-year

15
00:01:11,130 --> 00:01:15,195
loans to buy a home carried a rate of 7
percent or more.

16
00:01:16,730 --> 00:01:20,296
Nora isn't a real person; we built her
from typical figures,

17
00:01:20,296 --> 00:01:22,970
to show how the math works for one
household.

18
00:01:23,905 --> 00:01:28,486
She had moved for a new job and bought her
first home,

19
00:01:28,486 --> 00:01:34,085
with a $375,000 loan at 7.62 percent, the
average rate that month.

20
00:01:35,055 --> 00:01:38,115
Every dollar amount in this story is in
the dollars

21
00:01:38,115 --> 00:01:40,455
of the day, not adjusted for inflation.

22
00:01:41,990 --> 00:01:44,738
You may have heard that a refinance only
pays off once

23
00:01:44,738 --> 00:01:47,130
rates fall a full percentage point below
yours.

24
00:01:47,465 --> 00:01:49,575
We use that one-point line as a test.

25
00:01:49,985 --> 00:01:52,805
And for Nora, rates did cross that line.

26
00:01:53,395 --> 00:01:58,765
In 2026, the average spent 29 weeks at
least a full point below her rate.

27
00:01:59,035 --> 00:02:03,295
At the February low, the gap was 1.64
points.

28
00:02:03,835 --> 00:02:07,745
After the week ending July 23, 2026, the
gap never

29
00:02:07,745 --> 00:02:11,655
again reached a full point, and the window
closed.

30
00:02:12,355 --> 00:02:16,060
Whether it opens again, we can't tell you:
everything in this

31
00:02:16,060 --> 00:02:19,705
story is history, not a forecast, and it
covers the US only.

32
00:02:21,330 --> 00:02:23,430
So the letter arrives in a different
world.

33
00:02:23,925 --> 00:02:28,626
Say the rate it offers matches the
national average for

34
00:02:28,626 --> 00:02:32,815
the week ending September 24, 2026: 7.03
percent.

35
00:02:33,725 --> 00:02:37,485
That is just over half a percentage point
below what she pays now.

36
00:02:38,235 --> 00:02:40,698
From here on, when we say a point,

37
00:02:40,698 --> 00:02:44,755
we mean exactly that: one percentage point
off the rate.

38
00:02:45,745 --> 00:02:48,905
Her monthly payment would drop by $221.

39
00:02:50,460 --> 00:02:51,910
Then comes the last line.

40
00:02:52,785 --> 00:02:58,918
$5,124 in loan costs, paid in cash at
closing: the lender's charges for

41
00:02:58,918 --> 00:03:04,965
making the loan, plus anything she pays up
front to buy the rate down.

42
00:03:06,095 --> 00:03:07,775
That bill is not made up.

43
00:03:07,895 --> 00:03:12,488
It is the median from nearly half a
million refinances

44
00:03:12,488 --> 00:03:16,315
in 2025, and her loan size is the median
too.

45
00:03:17,295 --> 00:03:21,245
If a letter like this has reached you,
this is the math behind it.

46
00:03:22,740 --> 00:03:23,890
Is it worth paying?

47
00:03:25,085 --> 00:03:28,150
Worth it, here, means one thing: the
savings

48
00:03:28,150 --> 00:03:31,215
pay back the fees before she sells or
moves.

49
00:03:32,145 --> 00:03:36,105
We test a stay of three years, a choice we
made, not a rule.

50
00:03:36,575 --> 00:03:38,505
There are two quick answers.

51
00:03:38,745 --> 00:03:40,565
The one-point line says no.

52
00:03:41,045 --> 00:03:42,195
Her cut falls short.

53
00:03:43,015 --> 00:03:46,664
Simple division says yes:

54
00:03:46,664 --> 00:03:52,795
$5,124 divided by $221 is about 24 months.

55
00:03:54,125 --> 00:03:55,125
Neither is quite right.

56
00:03:55,725 --> 00:03:57,965
The one-point line never looks at the
bill.

57
00:03:58,375 --> 00:04:00,425
And the division leaves something out.

58
00:04:01,890 --> 00:04:02,890
Back to the letter.

59
00:04:03,095 --> 00:04:06,315
Break-even is the month when what she has
saved covers the bill.

60
00:04:06,995 --> 00:04:11,445
Division stacks up $221 a month and stops
at month 24.

61
00:04:12,365 --> 00:04:16,855
But while the savings stack up, something
else is happening, to what she still owes.

62
00:04:18,300 --> 00:04:23,510
Nora has made 35 payments on her old loan,
one a month since she moved in.

63
00:04:24,505 --> 00:04:27,975
With each one, a little more goes to
paying down what she owes.

64
00:04:28,815 --> 00:04:33,161
A refinance starts a fresh 30-year clock,
and in the early years

65
00:04:33,161 --> 00:04:36,895
of any mortgage, most of each payment goes
to interest.

66
00:04:37,765 --> 00:04:42,555
So the new loan pays down her balance more
slowly than the old one would have.

67
00:04:43,505 --> 00:04:47,105
By month 24, she owes $1,133 more

68
00:04:47,105 --> 00:04:50,815
than if she had kept the old loan.

69
00:04:51,855 --> 00:04:56,715
If she sold the house then, that $1,133
would come out of

70
00:04:56,715 --> 00:05:01,575
her sale money, so it cancels part of what
she has saved.

71
00:05:02,555 --> 00:05:05,380
Her savings need a few more months to
cover it,

72
00:05:05,380 --> 00:05:08,325
and the gap keeps growing a little while
they do.

73
00:05:09,445 --> 00:05:14,225
Count it, and break-even moves from month
24 to month 30.

74
00:05:15,750 --> 00:05:18,770
The smaller the cut, the more that gap
matters.

75
00:05:19,865 --> 00:05:22,485
Suppose her offer were only a quarter of a
point.

76
00:05:23,145 --> 00:05:25,725
Division promises break-even in 38 months.

77
00:05:26,535 --> 00:05:30,225
But a saving that small can't outrun the
growing gap in what she owes.

78
00:05:31,175 --> 00:05:36,705
Counting it, the fees never come back, not
in all the years her old loan had left.

79
00:05:38,110 --> 00:05:42,830
At a full point, she would break even in
18 months, well inside three years.

80
00:05:44,230 --> 00:05:47,480
Somewhere between a quarter point and a
full point is her line.

81
00:05:48,095 --> 00:05:52,225
At half a point, the fees come back in
exactly 36 months.

82
00:05:52,855 --> 00:05:58,405
So for Nora, the answer is half a point,
half of the one-point line.

83
00:05:58,955 --> 00:06:01,765
Her real offer, just over half a point,
clears it.

84
00:06:02,575 --> 00:06:05,622
The February window closed, but by the
three-year

85
00:06:05,622 --> 00:06:07,985
test, this letter still pays her back.

86
00:06:08,775 --> 00:06:10,965
Is half a point the line for everyone?

87
00:06:12,550 --> 00:06:15,190
Walt is another borrower we built from
typical figures.

88
00:06:15,875 --> 00:06:18,722
He bought a smaller house the same month
as

89
00:06:18,722 --> 00:06:21,635
Nora, with a $115,000 loan at the same
rate.

90
00:06:22,275 --> 00:06:23,945
His letter offers the same cut.

91
00:06:24,935 --> 00:06:29,295
The bill at the bottom: $3,667.

92
00:06:29,995 --> 00:06:31,725
His loan is far smaller than hers.

93
00:06:32,205 --> 00:06:34,045
His bill is only a little smaller.

94
00:06:35,590 --> 00:06:40,840
In the 2025 data, refinance bills grow far
more slowly than the loans they come with.

95
00:06:41,705 --> 00:06:45,215
For Walt, it comes to about 3.2 percent of
what he borrowed.

96
00:06:46,125 --> 00:06:48,435
His savings, though, shrink with the loan.

97
00:06:49,325 --> 00:06:52,375
The same cut takes only $68 a month off
his payment.

98
00:06:53,245 --> 00:06:58,425
$68 a month, against a $3,667 bill.

99
00:06:59,435 --> 00:07:02,575
Counting what he owes, he breaks even in
month 75.

100
00:07:04,020 --> 00:07:10,140
If Walt sold after three years, he would
come out $1,777 behind.

101
00:07:10,865 --> 00:07:13,983
For his fees to come back within three
years,

102
00:07:13,983 --> 00:07:16,755
his rate would need to fall 1.12 points.

103
00:07:17,695 --> 00:07:19,815
That is more than the one-point line.

104
00:07:21,005 --> 00:07:24,255
So that is why a smaller mortgage needs a
bigger rate cut.

105
00:07:25,175 --> 00:07:27,336
The fees barely shrink with the loan,

106
00:07:27,336 --> 00:07:30,315
and the monthly savings shrink right along
with it.

107
00:07:31,840 --> 00:07:33,260
Anjali sits at the other end.

108
00:07:33,895 --> 00:07:36,535
Same month, same rate, same cut.

109
00:07:37,565 --> 00:07:43,021
She bought a bigger house, with a $655,000
loan, still under the conforming limit:

110
00:07:43,021 --> 00:07:47,745
the ceiling for the standard loans that
Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac buy.

111
00:07:48,835 --> 00:07:53,435
Her bill is $5,514, close to what Nora
pays.

112
00:07:54,335 --> 00:08:00,075
If she sold after three years, she would
come out $5,250 ahead.

113
00:08:01,105 --> 00:08:03,460
To get her fees back within three years,

114
00:08:03,460 --> 00:08:06,345
she needs a cut of only about a third of a
point.

115
00:08:07,870 --> 00:08:10,770
So, back to the letter, and the question
it started with.

116
00:08:11,805 --> 00:08:14,971
How big a rate cut makes a refinance worth
it,

117
00:08:14,971 --> 00:08:18,825
if worth it means the fees come back
within three years?

118
00:08:20,065 --> 00:08:21,565
It depends on the size of the loan.

119
00:08:22,285 --> 00:08:27,105
For Walt, with a loan of $115,000, it
takes more than a full point.

120
00:08:27,855 --> 00:08:31,775
For Nora, with $375,000, half a point.

121
00:08:32,545 --> 00:08:36,985
For Anjali, with $655,000, about a third
of a point.

122
00:08:38,015 --> 00:08:42,275
Put your own loan on that scale, and you
can see which of them you are closest to.

123
00:08:43,135 --> 00:08:47,314
Those lines rest on our assumptions: a
loan taken out at the

124
00:08:47,314 --> 00:08:51,215
October 2023 rate, a median bill, and a
three-year stay.

125
00:08:52,075 --> 00:08:54,135
The one-point line fits none of them.

126
00:08:55,700 --> 00:09:00,190
These are national average rates, median
bills, and three made-up borrowers.

127
00:09:01,195 --> 00:09:05,868
In 2025, half of refinance bills

128
00:09:05,868 --> 00:09:10,395
fell between $3,443 and $8,270.

129
00:09:10,875 --> 00:09:14,075
A real rate, a real bill and a real
balance will be different.

130
00:09:14,895 --> 00:09:17,597
Paying the fees by adding them to the
loan,

131
00:09:17,597 --> 00:09:20,675
or choosing a shorter term, changes the
math too.

132
00:09:22,230 --> 00:09:26,690
Which leaves the one number only you know:
how long you will stay.

133
00:09:27,945 --> 00:09:33,449
For Nora, the same offer comes out $1,039
ahead if she sells

134
00:09:33,449 --> 00:09:38,495
after three years, and $8,093 ahead if she
stays seven.

135
00:09:39,605 --> 00:09:42,185
The rate cut decides how fast the fees
come back.

136
00:09:42,785 --> 00:09:46,665
How long you stay decides whether you are
still there when they do.

137
00:09:48,315 --> 00:09:50,305
How long do you picture yourself in your
home?

